Z||000000||wxxo||07-12-2000||12:10 PM||||is it possible to paint the interion of a fiero. like the dash etc?? if so what type of paint can you use. got some sears weatherbeater in the basement. haha. seriously i hate this brown color so bad that i have taken almost all of it out.tia||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||07-12-2000||12:15 PM||||The 2 experts I know of are WT III and 87ECO5 (87EC5?  I can never remember).  I think WT III has repainted his entire interior and I know that Scott (87ECsomething) has at least expiramented (sp?) on an armrest.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bill Osmer||07-12-2000||12:21 PM||||You can "paint" the interior vinyl components with vinyl dyes (avaialble from your local auto parts stores. Carpeting will require fabric dyes, as well as any other fabrics (seat covers).<P>I've done some touch up with grays and blacks, and they apear to not only be agood match, but also fairly durable.<P>IMHO<P>------------------<BR>Bill Osmer<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumcardsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-12-2000||12:54 PM||||u can paint any interior part. Auto paint suppliers have it. The best is brand called 'Mar-Hyde' others are ok too. Its what manufacturers use, comes ready mixed to spray. Its also great for vinyl tops, and ive even done seats.<P>rg<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||LAMBO||07-12-2000||12:57 PM||||Before you paint (dye) its very important to clean the surfaces with cleaner thats that recommended by the paint manufacturer and that you use the special primer they recommend also. If you don't, the paint will start to flake off very soon after you've finished painting. I've learned the hard way. Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fotofrank||07-12-2000||01:31 PM||||Bill, will the shade of gray you used match the '84 interior gray? Who makes it and what is the code or collor name?<BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1.html||||reg||5
Z||000006||terryk||07-12-2000||01:46 PM||||If you use vinyl paint of the correct color code (from your RPO label), use a prep cleaner (like Pre-Sol or Prep-Clean), and use a good clear coat, you will have parts that look new and match. Quite amazing. <P>On my Ciera GT, I had to use grey interior pieces to restore it. I painted the parts the correct code 79 color using the proper paint and preparation and they look factory. You don't have to have the right color part. Just paint it. In fact many GM interior parts come in charcoal as "paint to match".<P>Don't shoot anymore coats than is need to color the part or you do risk filling in the texture. I've found that two coats usually does the trick. For $50-60 you can paint all of the parts. <P>The dash is painted the lighter color first, then the top is painted the darker color. If you look, you will see the seam just under the front edge.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||07-12-2000||08:09 PM||||Is there a difference in vinyl/plastic Paint and vinyl dye? I know of leather dye, the stuff that's like ink.<P><BR>LAMBO says:before you *paint (dye)??<BR>Bill said: you can *paint??<BR>wxxo said: is it possoble to *paint??<BR>Terryk said: if you use vinyl *paint??<P>So is it paint, or is it dye?<P>THANKS<BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||5
Z||000008||mrfixit58||07-12-2000||09:06 PM||||DXR_DAD,<P>Now your asking something I know a little about. The term dye/paint is interchangable. The paint is high cover paint. As mentioned by others, clean, prep, spray. If you can use a spray gun, you would be surprised how easy it is to make you interior look like new.<P>Just like you've said... just do it!<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||07-12-2000||10:35 PM||||Thanks Roy, I don't think I'll get into the right now. Just wanted the info(Just in Case)<P>I'm doing a re-glue on the center console of a friends fiero and this info my come in handy.<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||07-12-2000||11:30 PM||||Loki painted the inside of his 85 GT grey... it was originally tan, but I couldn't tell.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <A HREF="http://www.cox-internet.com/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cox-internet.com/fiero/</A> <P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A> <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Loki||07-13-2000||12:16 AM||||WWAAZZAAUUPP!!!!!!!<P>    I changed my intier interior to a light gray. Dont have can right at the moment, but I got it at Advanced Auto, and it matches the light gray perfectly. Has been real durable and if it needed to be touched up it would be even easier. I took all peices out including dash.  cleaned with a nice vinyl cleaner and a scrub brush. Washed it off and let it dry for a day. Then I appled in total about 4 coats of vinyl paint. Looks awsome....DarkLord was almost right, the whole interior wasnt all tan, but most of it was.... Just follow the link to see a few pics..<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cox-internet.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.cox-internet.com/fiero</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||07-14-2000||02:44 PM||||technically, its a 'vinyl coating' it has additives to make it adhere better to plastic/vinyl parts that cant be sanded. it is very similar to laquer, dries very fast. Since its in a can, can be sprayed lots of people call it 'paint', even the paint salesmen call it interior paint. You can go to the supplier and pick out the correct color from a chart of all auto interiors or use the code from your car to match the original. it can be flat(matt), semi-flat, or glossy, i dont recommend using the gloss top coat except on seats, parts will reflect in your glass.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-14-2000).]||||reg||1
